A strict MVP should therefore implement only the **minimum end-to-end path** that proves the concept. The most defensible cut is:

| Priority | Scope |
|---|---|
| Must-have | CMDB/CSDM topology backbone, Prometheus/OpenShift live runtime signals, AppDynamics application/tier/node model, MongoDB authoritative role signal, snapshot API, single UI |
| Strong stretch | MSSQL authoritative role signal, Compute-VM signals via AppDynamics, Kafka traffic posture, IBM MQ traffic posture |
| Contract-only or mock-first | Oracle, NetApp ONTAP, AVI load balancer, object/file storage, Batch-Autosys |

The screenshots also define availability levels. To make them operationally useful, the platform should map them into a **source capability prior**, not a permanent truth ceiling. This matters because the brief explicitly encourages discovering new deterministic sources. If a better authoritative source is found later, the prior should be lifted.

| Brief level | Meaning in the screenshots | Source capability prior | How it affects confidence |
|---|---|---:|---|
| 1 | No telemetry or data available | 0.00 | No direct runtime answer from that source; usually yields **UNKNOWN** |
| 2 | Telemetry exists in a proprietary tool, not enterprise observability | 0.65 | Usable through a direct adapter, but correlation risk is higher |
| 3 | Available but not standardised | 0.85 | Usable with identifier reconciliation and stronger evidence checks |
| 4 | Available and standardised | 1.00 | Preferred source; no prior penalty |
| TBD | Source not yet identified | 0.40 | Candidate only; never sufficient alone for high confidence |

This mapping is a **platform rule**, not a fact from vendor docs. The brief itself states that the table indicates confidence in current data availability, not completeness of coverage, and that the table should evolve as teams discover additional sources.

