**Short explanation.** The adapters only collect and normalise observations. They do not decide truth. The correlation engine reads the evidence store, applies explicit rules, and writes immutable decision snapshots. The UI reads snapshots and evidence separately, which keeps the operator view clean and auditable.

This architecture aligns well with the official tool capabilities. OpenTelemetry Collector is explicitly designed to receive, process, and export telemetry; connectors bridge pipelines; the service-graph connector can infer inter-service relationships from traces, but those derived graphs should remain corroborative rather than authoritative. Prometheus exposes both time-series queries and metadata APIs; ServiceNow CMDB tracks CI relationships and dynamic application services; and OpenShift monitoring already bundles Prometheus, `kube-state-metrics`, `openshift-state-metrics`, and `node-exporter`. citeturn13view0turn13view1turn13view2turn17view3turn22view3turn21view0turn21view1turn14view0turn14view1turn14view2

Component responsibilities should be split as follows.

| Component | Responsibility | Hard rule |
|---|---|---|
| Source adapter | Pull source-native payloads and timestamps | Must not collapse semantics across technologies |
| Normalizer | Convert raw payload into canonical observation + claims | Must preserve provenance and raw payload hash |
| Canonical ID resolver | Map source-native IDs to internal keys and OTel attributes | Must never invent `service.instance.id` when ambiguous |
| Evidence store | Persist raw observations, claims, and provenance | Append-only for observations |
| Correlation engine | Compute snapshots and confidence | Rules-based only, no free-form inference |
| Snapshot store | Persist immutable point-in-time answers | Must link back to evidence |
| API | Serve snapshots, evidence, diffs, discoveries | Contract-first, OpenAPI 3.1 |
| UI | Show single app view + evidence drill-down | No raw-telemetry-first UX |
| Discovery registry | Record newly discovered deterministic sources | Must support sharing and audit |

For the correlation store, PostgreSQL is the best MVP choice. Its recursive `WITH` queries are explicitly suited to hierarchical or tree-structured data. Neo4j becomes attractive later when variable-length path traversal and graph-first exploration dominate. Neptune is attractive when you specifically want a fully managed graph service and are comfortable with AWS-specific operational boundaries. citeturn15view0turn9search1turn9search7turn16view1

The required comparison is below. The **ops complexity, cost, scaling, and dev speed** entries are an engineering assessment for this specific hackathon problem, based on the official feature and operating models.

| Row | PostgreSQL | Neo4j | Amazon Neptune |
|---|---|---|---|
| Query patterns | Excellent for fixed hierarchy, audit joins, time-window snapshots, recursive CTE traversals | Excellent for graph-native traversals, pattern matching, variable-length paths | Excellent for graph-native traversals; supports openCypher, Gremlin, and SPARQL |
| Ops complexity | Low | Medium if self-managed; lower if managed separately | Low on DB operations, medium on AWS/IAM/network integration |
| Cost | Lowest MVP baseline | Medium | Highest baseline |
| Scaling | Strong for transactional workloads and explicit hierarchies; weaker for deep graph-heavy exploration | Strong for graph-heavy traversal workloads | Strong managed HA and read scaling |
| Dev speed | Fastest for this brief’s explicit hierarchy | Fast once graph model dominates | Slower for a 48-hour hackathon because of cloud setup and service boundaries |

**Recommendation:** use **PostgreSQL for the MVP**, then add optional graph projection in Phase 2. PostgreSQL supports recursive hierarchical queries directly; Neo4j’s Cypher is purpose-built for graph pattern matching and variable-length paths; and Neptune is fully managed and supports open graph APIs, but brings more environment setup and service coupling than this brief needs on day one. citeturn15view0turn9search7turn9search1turn16view1

## Data model, identifiers, evidence store, and correlation rules

The platform should keep **native identity**, **canonical identity**, and **OTel identity** separate.

| Identity field | Purpose | Rule |
|---|---|---|
| `source_native_id` | Preserve vendor/source identity | Never discard |
| `application_key` | Stable internal application key | Prefer CMDB application-service or business-application identity |
| `component_key` | Stable internal component key | `application_key + component_name` |
| `asset_key` | Stable infrastructure key | `source_system + native_asset_id` |
| `location_key` | Stable site/region key | Explicit site/DC/zone mapping table |
| `service.namespace` | OTel grouping namespace | Use when available |
| `service.name` | OTel logical service name | Use when available |
| `service.instance.id` | OTel unique service-instance identity | Use only when source provides it or it is unambiguous to derive |

The OTel rule is important. The official semantic conventions say `service.instance.id` must be unique within the `service.namespace, service.name` pair, and collectors should not set it unless they can **unambiguously** determine the service instance. They also note that scraping receivers often can do that because they know the target address and port. citeturn12view0turn12view1turn12view2

A practical source-to-canonical mapping strategy is below.

| Source | Application mapping | Component mapping | Instance mapping | Location hint |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| CMDB/CSDM | application service / business app | CI or service child | usually not instance-level | CMDB location / region / site fields |
| AppDynamics | application id or name | tier name | node id | machine or node metadata |
| Prometheus/OpenShift | workload / service labels | deployment/statefulset/pod role | OTel attrs if present; else internal fingerprint from scrape target | node, zone, namespace labels |
| MongoDB | cluster / app tag | db component name | member `host:port` | site from host/location registry |
| MSSQL | AG name / app tag | sql component | replica server name | site from server/location registry |
| Oracle | db unique name / app tag | oracle component | db host + sid/service | site from host/location registry |
| Kafka | cluster / topic ownership mapping | kafka component | broker id / client id context | site from broker registry |
| IBM MQ | queue manager / app mapping | mq component | queue manager name | site from queue manager registry |
| ONTAP | storage VM / volume mapping | storage component | ONTAP object UUID | site/cluster metadata |
| AVI | virtual service / pool mapping | ingress component | virtual-service UUID | site/controller mapping |

A compact PostgreSQL schema for the MVP is below.

```sql
CREATE TABLE source_system (
    id              BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    code            TEXT UNIQUE NOT NULL,          -- cmdb, appd, prom, mongo, mssql...
    name            TEXT NOT NULL,
    kind            TEXT NOT NULL,                 -- api, scrape, file, event
    active          BOOLEAN NOT NULL DEFAULT TRUE
);

CREATE TABLE location (
    id              BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    location_key    TEXT UNIQUE NOT NULL,          -- dc1, dc2, ap-south-1a
    location_type   TEXT NOT NULL,                 -- datacenter, region, zone
    display_name    TEXT NOT NULL,
    parent_id       BIGINT REFERENCES location(id)
);

CREATE TABLE application (
    id              BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    application_key TEXT UNIQUE NOT NULL,
    display_name    TEXT NOT NULL,
    cmdb_ref        TEXT,
    owner_team      TEXT
);

CREATE TABLE component (
    id              BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    application_id  BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES application(id),
    component_key   TEXT UNIQUE NOT NULL,
    component_name  TEXT NOT NULL,
    component_type  TEXT NOT NULL,                 -- web, api, db, mq, kafka, lb
    intent_json     JSONB NOT NULL DEFAULT '{}'::jsonb
);

CREATE TABLE infrastructure_asset (
    id              BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    asset_key       TEXT UNIQUE NOT NULL,
    asset_type      TEXT NOT NULL,                 -- vm, pod, node, db_member, queue_manager, vs
    display_name    TEXT NOT NULL,
    native_source   TEXT NOT NULL,
    native_id       TEXT NOT NULL,
    location_id     BIGINT REFERENCES location(id),
    metadata_json   JSONB NOT NULL DEFAULT '{}'::jsonb
);

CREATE TABLE observation (
    id                      BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    observation_key         TEXT UNIQUE NOT NULL,
    source_system_id        BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES source_system(id),
    source_object_type      TEXT NOT NULL,
    source_object_id        TEXT NOT NULL,
    subject_type            TEXT NOT NULL,         -- application, component, asset
    subject_key             TEXT NOT NULL,
    observed_at             TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL,
    collected_at            TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL,
    freshness_ttl_seconds   INTEGER NOT NULL,
    capability_level        TEXT NOT NULL,         -- 1,2,3,4,TBD
    authoritative           BOOLEAN NOT NULL DEFAULT FALSE,
    raw_payload_hash        TEXT NOT NULL,
    raw_payload_json        JSONB NOT NULL
);

CREATE TABLE observation_claim (
    id                  BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    observation_id      BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES observation(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
    question            TEXT NOT NULL,            -- runtime_location, traffic_posture, state_authority
    predicate           TEXT NOT NULL,            -- runs_on, active_in, role, writable_in
    value_text          TEXT,
    location_id         BIGINT REFERENCES location(id),
    asset_id            BIGINT REFERENCES infrastructure_asset(id),
    support_type        TEXT NOT NULL,            -- supports, conflicts, missing
    authority_weight    NUMERIC(4,3) NOT NULL,
    metadata_json       JSONB NOT NULL DEFAULT '{}'::jsonb
);

CREATE TABLE decision_snapshot (
    id                  BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    snapshot_key        TEXT UNIQUE NOT NULL,
    application_id      BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES application(id),
    evaluated_at        TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL,
    window_start        TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL,
    window_end          TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL,
    summary_json        JSONB NOT NULL
);

CREATE TABLE decision_snapshot_item (
    id                  BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    decision_snapshot_id BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES decision_snapshot(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
    question            TEXT NOT NULL,
    component_id        BIGINT REFERENCES component(id),
    location_id         BIGINT REFERENCES location(id),
    answer_json         JSONB NOT NULL,
    confidence_raw      NUMERIC(5,2) NOT NULL,
    confidence_band     TEXT NOT NULL,            -- HIGH, MEDIUM, LOW, UNKNOWN
    status              TEXT NOT NULL             -- OK, STALE, CONFLICT, UNKNOWN
);

CREATE TABLE decision_snapshot_evidence (
    id                      BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    decision_snapshot_item_id BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES decision_snapshot_item(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
    observation_id          BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES observation(id),
    observation_claim_id    BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES observation_claim(id),
    evidence_role           TEXT NOT NULL,        -- primary, corroborative, conflicting, missing
    effective_weight        NUMERIC(5,3) NOT NULL
);

CREATE TABLE discovery_submission (
    id                  BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    submitted_at        TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
    submitted_by        TEXT NOT NULL,
    source_system_name  TEXT NOT NULL,
    signal_type         TEXT NOT NULL,
    stack_name          TEXT NOT NULL,
    justification       TEXT NOT NULL,
    shared_reference    TEXT,
    status              TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'PROPOSED'
